# Problem 1: Signal + Noise (34 points)

In class, we discussed how the least-squares estimators $\hat{\beta}_0$ and $\hat{\beta}_1$ can be written as a "signal + noise" decomposition. In this problem, we'll study that phenomenon.

Throughout this problem, assume that the Normal simple linear regression model holds, such that

$$Y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_i + \epsilon_i, \text{  for  } i=1,\dots,n$$
where $\epsilon_i | X \stackrel{iid}{\sim} N(0, \sigma^2)$.

## PART A (17pts)

Show that the ``signal + noise'' decomposition we discussed in class for the least squares estimators holds. In other words, show that:

\begin{align*}
\hat{\beta}_1 &= \beta_1 + \frac{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \epsilon_i (X_i - \bar{X})}{\widehat{\text{Var}}_n(X)}, \text{ and } \\
\hat{\beta}_0 &= \beta_0 + \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \epsilon_i \left\{ 1 - \frac{\overline{X} (X_i-\overline{X})}{\widehat{\text{Var}}_n(X)} \right\}
\end{align*}

where $\widehat{\text{Var}}_n(X) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X})^2$. As always, be sure to explain your work step-by-step for how you arrived at your answer. 

**Hint**: We've previously noted that $\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X}) \bar{Y} = 0$. As a result, $\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X})(Y_i - \bar{Y}) = \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X})Y_i$. For this problem, it will be useful for you to understand why these results are true. Furthermore, it will be useful to first prove the above result for $\hat{\beta}_1$, and then use that result to work with $\hat{\beta}_0$. 


