# Problem 2: Analyzing Election Data (66 points)

In this problem, you'll work with data I've seen in my own research. Here is the dataset:

```{r}
electionData = read.csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zjbranson/36401_fall2026/refs/heads/main/electionData.csv")
```

The dataset contains information on 1,005 mayoral elections that happened in Louisiana. Thus, each row in the dataset corresponds to a mayoral election, and we have information about each election and the municipality where it occurred. (A "municipality" is a city, town, or similar community.) Here are the variables that are available for each election:

+ `year`: The year that the election occurred.
+ `numCandidates`: The number of candidates that ran for mayor during the election.
+ `africanAmerican`: Were any of the candidates African American? (0 = no, 1 = yes).
+ `female`: Were any of the candidates female? (0 = no, 1 = yes).
+ `hs_pct`: Proportion of the municipality's population that has a high school degree.
+ `poverty`: Proportion of the municipality's population whose income is below the poverty line.
+ `unemp`: Proportion of the municipality's population that is unemployed.
+ `voter_turnout`: The percentage of eligible voters in the municipality who voted in the election.

One of the main goals of the study was to assess to what extent voter turnout was affected by the demographics of the candidates in that election and of the municipality. For this problem, we'll consider two regression models:

+ **Poverty-Degree Regression**: Regress `poverty` (as the outcome) onto `hs_pct` (as the covariate).

+ **Voter-Poverty Regression**: Regression `voter_turnout` (as the outcome) onto `poverty` (as the covariate).

The purpose of these regression models is to understand how poverty rates are related to high school degree rates, and how voter turnout is related to poverty rates.

## PART A (16pts)

For this part, implement the **Poverty-Degree Regression** and the **Voter-Poverty Regression** mentioned at the beginning of this problem. Then, for *each* regression, create 1-2 graphs that allow you to assess *all* of the assumptions involved in the regression model. Finally, based on your graph(s), discuss the plausibility of each regression model's assumptions in 1-4 sentences.

```{r}
library(dplyr)

povertyDegree <- lm(poverty ~ hs_pct, data = electionData)
voterPoverty <- lm(voter_turnout ~ poverty, data = electionData)

library(ggplot2)

ggplot(electionData, aes(x = hs_pct, y = poverty)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE, color = "blue") +
  labs(title = "Poverty vs HS %", x = "HS %", y = "Poverty") +
  theme_minimal()

qqnorm(residuals(povertyDegree)); qqline(residuals(povertyDegree))

ggplot(electionData, aes(x = poverty, y = voter_turnout)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE, color = "red") +
  labs(title = "Voter Turnout vs Poverty", x = "Poverty", y = "Turnout") +
  theme_minimal()

qqnorm(residuals(voterPoverty)); qqline(residuals(voterPoverty))
```

**To assess a good distribution for the residuals, we can look at their variance and overall placement in a standard scatterplot/regression line graph, and to assess normality we can use the q-q plot. For the first regression, the points on the graph compared to the line seem good, as most of them form a sort of oval shape with the lm line in the middle. With the QQplot, the residuals look pretty normal-distributed, with minor and likely negligible movement at the very ends of the graphs. With the second graph, there's no immediate warning like a curvy scatterplot distribution, but the line looks a little high compared to the majority of the points especially near 0.4 and 0.2. Also, at the right side of the QQplot the points seem to curve off in a more pronounced way from the line, so my conclusion is that we should investigate this one further, but it may be okay.**

